{"name":"Prophetic Purple","hex":"#624f59","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"prophetic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the ability to prophesize; prescient.","Of, or relating to a prophecy or a prophet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prophetic"},{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prophecy","blocks":[{"text":"In religion, mythology, and fiction, a prophecy is a message that has been communicated to a person by a supernatural entity. Prophecies are a feature of many cultures and belief systems and usually contain divine will or law, or preternatural knowledge, for example of future events. They can be revealed to the prophet in various ways depending on the religion and the story, such as visions, or direct interaction with divine beings in physical form. Stories of prophetic deeds sometimes receive considerable attention and some have been known to survive for centuries through oral tradition or as religious texts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Olaus_Magnus_-_On_the_Art_of_Prophecy.jpg/330px-Olaus_Magnus_-_On_the_Art_of_Prophecy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prophecy","wikidata":"Q214267"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Miriam_IMG_28071.JPG/500px-Miriam_IMG_28071.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Miriam_IMG_28071.JPG/1280px-Miriam_IMG_28071.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Miriam_IMG_28071.JPG/500px-Miriam_IMG_28071.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Miriam, prophetess in the Book of Exodus. 20th century mosaic in the Abbey of the Dormition","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiriam_IMG_28071.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Saul_1878.jpg/500px-Saul_1878.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Saul_1878.jpg/500px-Saul_1878.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"David and Saul, detail from an 1878 oil painting, Nationalmuseum, Stockholm","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaul_1878.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Ghent_Altarpiece_D_-_Popes_-_Bishops